I would like to create a production management web page for the game Satisfactory.
Users will be able to set up a production configuration how they like, and store it in browser
local storage or download the configuration as JSON to be used later.

I would like to use javascript, css and html. the javascript files should be divided into files that deal with the UI and files that model the user state. I want to be able to write tests that can be run for node against the files that model the user state.

You can choose the coding style you wish to use but I would like to see documentation for public functions that includes valid inputs and outputs and explains any side effects of that function.

MODEL

For the model I want to start with some immutable data structures. These should be filled from information looked up about the game Satisfactory. When serializing user state to JSON, reference elemental list items by their array index. When the UI represents items with options in selects, those values should be the array index. At runtime, you may use direct object references for convenience. There are lists that are elemental, and lists that are subset of the elemental lists. 

Notes:

  - All rates are units per minute
  - Power is measured in MegaWatt
  - clock speed has a valid range from 0-250%
  - different machines have different max sommersloops that can be looked up at https://satisfactory.wiki.gg/wiki/Somersloop for per machine values
  - HUB biomass burners have different stats than regular biomass burners but take the same fuelds as regular biomass burners.
  - for machines that produce power, they have specific fuels they may take. links to these can be found here https://satisfactory.wiki.gg/wiki/Buildings in the Power / Generators area. There are 6 power generating building types. The information about the fuel types is in a bullet list in a section with a h2 header 'Fuel'. for example, biomass burner can burn leaves, mycelia, wood, biomass, solid biofuel and packaged liquid biofuel.

The elemental lists are as follows:

 * recipes: these are satisfactory production recipes including alternate recipies.
    - name
    - machine: reference to a machine object that this recipe uses (see below)
    - default production rate
    - inputs: item -> default input rate
    - is_alternative: true/false
    
 * belts:
    - level
    - flow rate

 * machines - anything that takes inputs and/or produces outputs of either items or power
    - name
    - produces_power: true/false
    - allowed_fuels: fuel -> output energy
    - default power input
    - default power output
    - max number of sommersloops

 * items - anything that can be an input or output to a machine or recipe, or a fuel that a power producing machine can use as an input.
    - name
    - is_fluid: true/false
    - is_ore: true/false
    - is_fuel: true/false
    - radioactive_decay: also called ItemDecay, a measure used to calculate radiation level (see https://satisfactory.wiki.gg/wiki/Radiation)

The subset lists are as follows:

 * alternative recipies: list of recipes that are alternative

 * fluids - list of items that are fluids

 * powerplants - list of machines that are power plants
 
 * extractors - miners or extractors

 * primaries - items that are ores, water, nitrogen or crude oil

 * fuels - items that are fuels

 * production - machines that output items


State variables:

  these are variables that hold state for the user. These are saved and loaded as described above.

  * Unlocked Recipies: a list of recipes that have been unlocked. use an array of true/false where the indexes correspond to the alternative recipe subset list

  * Power Grids - a list that is initially populated with a default power grid. This default one will start with two HUB Biomass Burners.
    
  * Power Grid
    - name
    - Power Configuration
    - total power produced (calculated)
    - load: total power used by each production line connected to it (calculated)
    - fuel suppliers: list of production lines that supply fuel to this power grid, broken down by fuel type and amount consumed. These are set up by the user
    - reserve: number of power storage units
    - reserve time: how many seconds this grid can supply its load with only its reserve

  * Power Configuration: set and updated by the user.
    - powerplant type
    - fuel type used
    - clock speed
    - input items -> input rates (calculated)
    - produced power rate (calculated)
    - number of powerplants in this configuration

  * Production Lines - a list that is initially populated with a default production line named 'HUB line'.

  * Production Line
    - power grids: list of power grids used to supply this line, selected by user.
    - max power supplied: calculated maximum power possible by what the grids can supply
    - fuel outputs (calculated)
        * fuel type / rate / destination power grid
    - belt version used. this is set by the user. This caps the rate of input and produced items by the power configurations here. This is enforced by the calculations.
    - production goal: item -> target production rate. this is set by the user.
    - production machines: recipes -> production configurations (see below). this is set by the user
    - shortfall: items -> unfulfilled rates as calculated by the production goals and current production machines. this includes unfulfilled prerequisites of the items calculated recursively. For example: if you want Reinforced Iron Plates but lack Iron Plates, Screws and Iron Ingots, the shortfall shows Reinforced Iron Plates, Iron Plates, Screws, Rods AND Iron Ingots.

  * Production Configuration
    - recipe
    - production machine referenced by recipe
    - clock speed
    - number of sommersloops used (integer)
    - input items -> input rates (calculated, may not exceed the speed of the belt version selected)
    - produced items -> production rates (calculated, may not exceed the speed of the belt version selected)
    - number of production buildings with this configuration (integer)
